{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Secondary Electron Detector}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} PIF7_D}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Secondary Electron Detector
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx7975\cellx11075\pard\intbl The Secondary Electron Detector (2) is mounted on the rear wall of the chamber.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The electrode  (6) [shown as a grid in the diagram] is provided in front, which is held on positive potential for secondary electron detection and forms a collecting field for secondary electrons around the specimen. They move towards the detector (6). 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The scintillator (5) is mounted on light guide (4) inside the chamber.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The scintillator (5)  - is biased to + 8 kV and accelerates the low-energy secondary electrons to a considerably higher energy level. These impinge on the scintillator (5) where they generate photons which the light guide (4) directs out of the chamber to photomultiplier (3). \cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm29.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par Photomultiplier (3) amplifies and converts the light current into electron current which represents the secondary video signal on the output of the preamplifier. If the collector is biased to negative potential only backscattered electrons will fall on the scintillator. A backscattered electron image of the specimen surface is produced in this operating mode.
\par 
\par This detector is especially suitable for long working distances. The lateral "illumination" emphasizes the topography. 
\par \pard 
\par \page
