{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Depth of Penetration}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Depth_of_Penetration}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \fs36\cf1 Depth of Penetration
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The higher the high voltage the deeper the penetration, or, the lower the atomic number of the element at the specimen spot the deeper the penetration.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b a)\plain\fs24 \tab If the electron beam penetrates deeply into the specimen, more information stems from deeper specimen layers than from the surface.
\par 
\par \b b)\plain\fs24 \tab The beam is expanded by scattering when penetrating deeply into the specimen, which impairs the spatial resolution.
\par 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b First conclusion\plain\fs24 : High accelerating voltages are useless for chemically "light" material (organic specimens) unless the specimen surface is "sealed" by a metal (gold) layer which will supply the information. 
\par 
\par \b Second conclusion\plain\fs24 : Sensitive specimens destroyed by the energy absorbed from the beam require a lower potential to reduce the energy. 
\par \tab \b Exception\plain\fs24 : 
\par \tab Thin foils and membranes where high voltage will transmit the foil so that it absorbs less energy than at low voltage when it absorbs the whole beam.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par \b Third conclusion\plain\fs24 : For observation of coated insulators the beam should not penetrate the coating (low voltage) to prevent charging of the insulator which is not discharged and could make imaging impossible.
\par \page
