{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Dual Magnification Principle}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Dual_Magnification_Principle}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Dual Magnification Principle
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx12685\pard\intbl In \b Dual Magnification Mode\plain\fs24  the left and right halves of the split image may have different magnifications.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The\b  left image\plain\fs24  is at base magnification and contains a rectangular \b raster frame\plain\fs24  (the zoom raster) which may be \ul dragged\plain\fs24 {\v Drag} with the mouse to change both it's size and position. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The right image shows the area covered by the zoom raster. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The \b aspect ratio\plain\fs24  of the zoom raster is fixed to that of the split image, as the raster is made smaller the magnification of the right image is correspondingly increased thus providing the zoom facility. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The zoom factor may be from 1.0 to 10.0.\cell\pard \pard\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm49.bmp\}
\par\intbl \cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par \page
