{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Floppy Disk Drive}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Floppy_Disk_Drive}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Floppy Disk Drive
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par Data are stored on disks on concentric tracks. The write/read head of the disk drive moves from track to track while the disk rotates, thereby searching data for reading or blank areas for writing.
\par 
\par The tracks are divided into sectors and bytes. The storage capacity of a disk is given by bytes. One byte is required to store one character. The number of tracks, sectors and bytes of a disk depend on the disk type.
\par 
\par One KByte corresponds to 1024 bytes, one MByte to 1024 KByte.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \b Loading and removing disks:
\par 
\par \{bmc bm20.bmp\}\tab Caution:\plain\fs24  \tab Do not remove disks from the drive as long as \b drive signal lamp\plain\fs24  (1) is on, to prevent disk or write/read head from being damaged.
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx10985\pard\intbl \b Loading \plain\fs24 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm3.bmp\} Load disk in drive
\par\intbl \{bmc bm3.bmp\} Carefully slide in disk as far as it will go
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b Removing \plain\fs24 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm3.bmp\} Operate \b disk eject key\plain\fs24  (2) to the lower right of the disk drive. \cell\pard \pard\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm60.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 
\par See also \tab \uldb \b Floppy Disk Types\plain\b\fs24 {\v Floppy_Disk_Types}\plain\fs24  and \uldb \b Formatting Floppy Disks\plain\b\fs24 {\v Formatting_Floppy_Disks}\plain\fs24 .
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Floppy Disk Types}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Floppy_Disk_Types}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 Floppy Disk Types
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 The 1.44 MB disk drive also accepts 3.5" disks formatted for 720 KB. 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx12115\pard\intbl If 3.5" disks are loaded in 3.5" drives of different systems (with different storage capacities), only 1.44 MB disks should be used, which are formatted for 720 KB.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl Disks with high storage capacity are designated HD (high density) (1) and characterized by two square recesses (2).
\par\intbl  
\par\intbl 3.5" disks with HD capacity, formatted for 1.44 MB, can only be used in 1.44 MB disk drives. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl Disks with lower storage capacity are designated DD (double density) and have only one recess. They may be formatted and used in an HD disk drive. \cell\pard \pard\intbl    \{bmc bm61.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par New disks must be formatted before use, i.e. prepared for the user's system. Formatting clears all data stored on the disk; the disk is ready to be used for other purposes. 
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx10985\pard\intbl \b Write protection\plain\fs24  
\par\intbl A write-protected disk can be read but not overwritten. This prevents formatting by mistake and overwriting of data. 
\par\intbl 3.5" disks are provided with a write protect slot in the upper corner. If the slot is open, writing is impossible. Closing the slot by shifting the plastic tongue overrides write protection. \cell\pard \pard\intbl 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl    \{bmc bm62.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par \page
