{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Magnification Principle}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Magnification_Principle}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 Magnification Principle
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 Deflection coils in the objective lens move the electron beam in a scan field over the specimen.
\par \pard\tx355 The digital control provides for point-by-point scanning of the specimen. 
\par \pard\tx355 The size of the scan field on the specimen is variable by adjusting the magnification. The magnification is defined by the ratio of the scan field size on the screen to the scan field size on the specimen.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx12685\pard\intbl 
\par\intbl If the scan field on the specimen is of the same size as the scan field on the screen, the magnification is 1x as shown in the diagram on the right.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl But if the scan field is only half this size, the specimen features on the monitor will be double the size of the previous adjustment.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The smaller the scan field on the specimen the higher the magnification.
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl                 \{bmc bm124.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par \page
