{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Reduced Raster Mode
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par A reduced scan area is provided inside the \uldb \b Image Window\plain\b\fs24 {\v Image_Window}\plain\fs24 . The image outside the scan area is frozen.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm17.bmp\}\tab Size and position may be adjusted:
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx7935\cellx12115\pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab \ul Click\plain\fs24 {\v Click} at the border line. \ul Handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles} appear.
\par\intbl \tab When you come close to the border line the cursor changes indicating you are in adjust mode (shape in top right corner).
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab Point to the border line away from the handles and adjust \b position\plain\fs24  of the scan area.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab Pick up a handle and adjust \b size\plain\fs24  of the scan area.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab Click inside the scan area to return to normal adjustment mode.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab Switch Reduced Raster Mode to OFF by selecting \uldb \b Normal Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Normal_Scan}\plain\fs24 .\cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl\tx355 \par\pard \pard\intbl         \{bmc bm152.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 
\par \{bmc bm12.bmp\}\tab Reduced raster may be automatically selected when \uldb \b focus wobble\plain\b\fs24 {\v Focus_Wobble}\plain\fs24  is switched on. See User preferences \uldb \b SEM Conditions\plain\b\fs24 {\v 9TLKMY@leoserv.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Resolution}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Resolution}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Resolution
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The higher the voltage the smaller the effective beam diameter of the specimen, because
\par \b 
\par a)\plain\fs24  the highly energized electromagnetic lenses have less aberrations,
\par 
\par \b b)\plain\fs24  the smaller relative chromatic width of the beam reduces the chromatic aberration,
\par 
\par \b c)\plain\fs24  the brightness of the source increases which means more electrons in the beam cross section,
\par 
\par \b d)\plain\fs24  the beam is deflected less from nominal position by magnetic stray fields.
\par 
\par It generally applies that the smaller the beam diameter the better the lateral resolution, unless this is precluded by any of the reasons mentioned in Section \uldb \b Depth of Penetration\plain\b\fs24 {\v Depth_of_Penetration}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
