{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Scan Rotation Principle}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Scan_Rotation_Principle}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Scan Rotation Principle
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par Scan Rotation rotates the scan field on the specimen, that means the specimen "illumination" rotates along with the specimen. The Scan Rotation axis always coincides with the electron-optical axis so that the specimen tilt does not influence the rotation.
\par Electrical Scan Rotation is a convenient means to align the edges of the scan field on the specimen. It may be used for better display, but is more important as alignment tool for line scan and measuring system, permitting scanning in adjustable direction without mechanical rotation, especially because mechanical alignment is the more critical the higher the magnification.
\par \pard 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx7935\cellx12685\pard\intbl \b\fs28 Circuit principle
\par\intbl \plain\fs24 Scan Rotation rotates the scan field on the specimen electronically, so that the specimen image rotates in the opposite direction on the monitor. The circuit transforms a right-angled coordinate system \b x / y\plain\fs24  into a right-angled coordinate system \b u / v\plain\fs24  turned about the angle \f3\b f\plain\fs24 , according to the following equations: \par\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \tab \tab \b\cf2 u\plain\b\fs24  = x cos \f3 f \f4 - y sin \f3 f\plain\fs24 
\par\intbl \b \tab \tab \cf2 v\plain\b\fs24  = x sin \f3 f \f4 - y cos \f3 f\plain\fs24 
\par\intbl Both coordinate systems have the same origin in the center of the scan field.
\par\intbl The trigonometric functions are generated digitally and graded in degrees. The deflection amplifiers for the electron beam are driven by the deflection voltages u / v after transformation of x / y into u / v.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl\tx355  
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl    \{bmc bm160.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 
\par \page
