{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Signal Generation
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx12685\pard\intbl\tx355 The narrowly bundled electron beam\b  PE\plain\fs24  (primary electron beam) generates different signals on the specimen surface:
\par\intbl \b SE\plain\fs24 \tab Secondary electrons from the topmost specimen layer 
\par\intbl \b BSE\plain\fs24 \tab Backscattered electrons from a depth of up to 1 \f3 m\f4 m.
\par\intbl \b CL\plain\fs24 \tab Cathodoluminescence of luminescent specimens
\par\intbl \b X\plain\fs24 \tab X-rays from a depth of approximately 1 \f3 m\f4 m
\par\intbl \b TE\plain\fs24 \tab Transmitted electrons from exceptionally thin specimens
\par\intbl \b SC\plain\fs24 \tab Absorbed electrons grounded as current 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The above, incomplete list shows that with suitable detectors a great amount of information can be derived from a specimen. Secondary Electrons are used primarily to image the specimen surface.
\par\intbl For \b SE\plain\fs24  detection see \uldb \b Secondary Detector\plain\b\fs24 {\v PIF7_D}\plain\fs24  
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl For detection of \b BSE\plain\fs24  see \uldb \b Back Scattered Electron Detector\plain\b\fs24 {\v 2AR7TXP@DlgMgr.HLP}.\plain\fs24 \cell\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 
\par\intbl      \{bmc bm167.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \b SC \plain\fs24 detection and measurement are described in 
\par \pard\tx355 \uldb \b Absorbed Specimen Current Measurement\plain\b\fs24 {\v JW692B@DlgMgr.HLP}\plain\fs24  and 
\par \pard\tx355 \uldb \b Beam Current Measurement\plain\b\fs24 {\v CL0TRY@DlgMgr.HLP}\plain\fs24 
