{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Tilt Correction Principle}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Tilt_Correction_Principle}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Tilt Correction Principle
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par In the scanning electron microscope all specimen features are projection displays, both on screen and photograph. Tilted surfaces are foreshortened in the tilt direction, that means they are in a way distorted compared with vertical observation direction. The distortion is corrected by shortening the scan field at right angles to the tilt axis and so stretching the image in this direction on screen and photograph, which provides for correction with proper adjustment. This can be achieved only with perfectly flat samples such as polished sections.
\par \pard 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx11555\pard\intbl If the electron beam moves over the specimen from\b  y1\plain\fs24  to \b y2\plain\fs24  it covers the distance \b L\plain\fs24 . Tilting the specimen from horizontal position about the angle \f3\b q\plain\f3\fs24  \f4 displays on the monitor the projection of the distance \b L\plain\fs24 , that is \b L\'92\plain\fs24 . The specimen is foreshortened by the factor
\par\intbl \par\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \tab \b L\'92/L = k = cos \f3 q\plain\fs24 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl Foreshortening by this factor the scan field on the specimen reestablishes on the monitor the original distance \b L\plain\fs24 . The field is foreshortened by adjustable attenuation of the Y amplitude of the deflection current.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl\tx355            \{bmc bm212.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 \{bmc bm12.bmp\}\tab Tilt Correction is effective in the Y direction, that means the image is rectified vertically on the screen. The tilt direction must run along the Y axis and the tilt axis of the specimen surface along the X axis. 
\par \page
