{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Use of Different Scan Modes}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Use_of_Different_Scan_Modes}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn\tx2385 \fs36\cf1 Use of Different Scan Modes
\par \pard\tx2385 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx2385 Scan time and scan mode selection can greatly influence screening procedure and the result of the action.
\par \pard\tx2385 
\par \pard\tx2385 Fast scans are used if the magnification and the specimen sensitivity permit the generation of a sufficiently strong and noiseless signal. It is used for quick adjustment and screening of specimens, because fast scan images follow immediately the mechanical movement of the specimen. This mode must also be used for dynamic processes, e.g. aperture alignment with focus wobble
\par \pard\tx2385 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx7935\cellx12685\pard\intbl 
\par\intbl Fast scans can help to produce an image of the surface for the examination of charging specimens, because the generated charge pattern is more homogeneous than in slow acquisition mode.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl A signal which is so low that it causes interfering noise will require longer scan times. The noise is then reduced by filtering. The longer the image acquisition time the higher the noise reduction.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The example on the right compares a scan at speed 13 Upper half of the image) and speed 1 (below) in line average mode.\cell\pard \pard\intbl         \{bmc bm218.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 
\par For further information see \tab \uldb \b Scanning Speed Adjustment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Scanning_Speed_Adjustment}
\par 
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \uldb Scan Mode Adjustment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Scan_Mode_Adjustment}
\par 
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \uldb Raster Type Selection\plain\b\fs24 {\v Raster_Type_Selection} 
\par 
\par \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 See also \tab \tab \tab \uldb \b Noise Reduction Principle\plain\b\fs24 {\v Noise_Reduction_Principle}.
