{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Vacuum System}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Vacuum_System}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \fs36\cf1 Vacuum System
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par \b 
\par \plain\fs24 The operation of the electron optical column and specimen scanning is dependent not only on the presence of a vacuum, but also the degree of vacuum, i.e. the system is safely interlocked so that it is not possible to switch on any of the high voltages until an adequate vacuum is reached.
\par 
\par The vacuum system is simple and efficient. A turbomolecular pump, backed by a rotary pump, is mounted on the lower face of the specimen chamber and commences pumping when the system is being evacuated and comes to rest during system venting. The electron gun and column are pumped through the tube running from the chamber up to the gun section. The only valve in this system is the air-admittance valve attached to the rear of the chamber, which automatically opens when venting is requested. Normal operating vacuum should be in the range to   10\fs16 -4 \fs24 to 10\fs16 -6\fs24  torr.  No water cooling is required.
\par \pard See \uldb \b SEM Control Vacuum\plain\b\fs24 {\v C9V9DR@tabmgr.hlp}
\par 
\par \plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Column and Chamber}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Column_and_Chamber}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Column and Chamber
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par 
\par The LEO 420/430 \ul \b\ul gun and column\plain\b\fs24 {\v 19A.J.D@maint.hlp}\plain\fs24  is used to generate and focus a fine beam of electrons onto the surface of a specimen mounted in the specimen chamber. The electron gun has a thermionic emitter as a source of electrons - the source is a tungsten hairpin filament. Three electromagnetic lenses beneath the gun, focus and shape the electron beam before it strikes the specimen in a scanned, or rastered, fashion. The energy of the electron beam is adjustable from 300 volts to 30 kilovolts in 10 volt steps and the electron probe current is continuously adjustable from 1 pico-amp to 1 micro-amp to suit the type of examination required.
\par \pard 
\par The specimen chamber of the LEO 430 holds the specimen to be viewed in such a way that it may be freely manoeuvred during examination. The specimen stage which holds the specimen is attached to the door of the chamber and is of the manual cartesian type on the LEO 420 and is a 5 axis motorised cartesian type on the LEO 430.  The stage may be opened for examination once the vacuum within the chamber has been vented.
\par 
\par 
